comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T06:05:38.614Z","harness:claude-code","Live reproduction 2026-07-26 on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, recorded as evidence for the acceptance clause 'a clean merge leaves pm validate with zero history-drift warnings'. Scratch repository, pm merge install applied, two branches diverging on one item: agentA adds a comment and sets priority=1, agentB adds a different comment and sets estimated_minutes=60. git merge exits 0 with no conflict; the field-aware driver reports fields_from_theirs=[estimated_minutes], union_fields=[comments], history strategy=union_reanchor entries_total=5; the merged item correctly carries priority 1, estimated_minutes 60, and both comments. Immediately afterwards pm history <id> --verify returns ok:false with errors [verify_failed:current_item_hash_mismatch], latest_after_hash f6b6065e... against current_item_hash a1c9cbfb..., and pm validate --check-history-drift reports drifted_items_count 1 with hash_mismatches 1. So a correct, conflict-free merge leaves the workspace failing its own integrity check with no warning at merge time. Filed pm-pdr8t1 for the detection half: CI's merge-safety step verifies one history stream of 2,058 and omits --check-history-drift entirely, so this drift would not be seen in CI even after it happens."
  "2026-07-26T11:46:22.561Z","harness:codex","Start evidence 2026-07-26: selected after live all-status review of 2,081 items (1,780 closed, 288 open, 13 canceled; zero pre-existing in-progress), full item metadata and shipped lineage review, and GitHub/security/dependency inventory. This item is actively implemented in the cohesive public SDK plus safe agent-fleet PR; container epics and unrelated backlog remain open and unclaimed."
  "2026-07-26T12:11:36.910Z","harness:codex","Investigation evidence: reviewed merge driver, three-way history union, reconcile, and history-repair contracts. The existing explicit reconcile path can append a replay-addressable repair event, but automatic post-merge coordination requires a hook/provenance design beyond this branch; no code was attributed to this item."
  "2026-07-26T13:11:38.981Z","harness:codex","Verification 2026-07-26: repository release gates passed build/typecheck/docs/static quality, SDK surface/import-cost/transport ratchets, secret scan, version policy, 374 files and 6,420 tests at exact 100% statements/branches/functions/lines, packed npx smoke, and 88-command package-first dogfood. Aggregate stopped only at hosted-analysis because the branch exact SHA was not yet pushed. Manual unrelated-directory packed consumer also proved the installed SDK link, 85-action coverage, same-harness cross-session claim exclusion, and bounded 22 MB Bun output capture."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T11:29:38.858Z","harness:claude-code","Post-merge drift reproduced on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, 2026-07-26, isolated sandbox.\n\nTwo branches mutated the same item (A: comment + priority; B: comment + tag). git merge auto-merged both artifacts and the merge driver's own output reported reanchored: true, then immediately emitted guidance to run pm validate --check-history-drift and pm history-repair --all. pm validate directly after the merge reported validate_history_drift_hash_mismatches:1. pm history-repair --all then returned repaired: 1, skipped_clean: 0, failed: 0, and validate came back clean.\n\nTwo things worth separating on this item.\n\nThe driver reports a reanchor it has not finished. reanchored: true and a hash mismatch one command later are contradictory as read by an agent: the operation announced success, and the very next verification says the chain is wrong. Whatever the internal distinction is between re-anchoring the stream and reconciling the item hash, the merge output does not carry it, so the honest state after a merge is unrepresented.\n\nThe remedy is a manual second step on a path that is otherwise fully automatic. A merge that resolves cleanly at the git level leaves the tracker in a state that fails its own validation until a human or an agent remembers to run a separate repair. Every branch merge in a multi-agent workflow hits this, and nothing in the git flow forces the second command.\n\nNote the contrast with the behaviour recorded on this item earlier: there, history-repair correctly declined with entries_rehashed: 0 because the merged field set had no entry to anchor to. Here repair succeeded. Both outcomes leave the same user-visible signature — a green merge followed by a red validate — which is an argument for the fix being in what the merge records rather than in what repair can recover."
